==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|recken}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|rekenen|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{term|recenian||to pay; arrange, dispose, reckon|lang=ang}} and {{term|gerecenian|ġerecenian|to explain, recount, relate|lang=ang}}; both from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*rekanōną||to count, explain}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*rekanaz||swift, ready, prompt}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*h₃roǵéye-||to straighten, direct}}. Cognate with {{etyl|sco|-}} {{term|rekkin||to ennumerate, mention, narrate, rehearse, count, calculate, compute|lang=sco}}, {{etyl|fy|-}} {{term|rekkenje||to account, tally, calculate, figure|lang=fy}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|rekenen||to count, calculate, reckon|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|nds|-}} {{term|rekenen||to reckon|lang=nds}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|rechnen||to count, reckon, calculate|lang=de}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|räkna||to count, calculate, reckon|lang=sv}}, {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|reikna||to figure|lang=is}}. See also {{l|en|reck}}, {{l|en|reach}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈɹɛkən/}}
* {{audio|en-us-reckon.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|ɛkən}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To count; to enumerate; to number; also, to compute; to calculate.
#* ...then the priest shall '''reckon''' unto him the money according to the years that remain... --''Lev. 27:18, King James Version''.
#: I '''reckoned''' above two hundred and fifty on the outside of the church. [[w:Joseph Addison|Joseph Addison]].
# To count as in a number, rank, or series; to estimate by rank or quality; to place by estimation; to account; to esteem; to repute.
#* He was '''reckoned''' among the transgressors. ''Luke 23:37, King James Version''
#* For him I '''reckon''' not in high estate. [[w:John Milton|John Milton]].
# To charge, attribute, or adjudge to one, as having a certain quality or value.
#* ...faith was '''reckoned''' to Abraham for righteousness. Romans 4:9, King James Version.
#* Without her eccentricities being '''reckoned''' to her for a crime. [[w:Nathaniel Hawthorne|Nathaniel Hawthorne]].
# To conclude, as by an enumeration and balancing of chances; hence, to think; to suppose; -- followed by an objective clause;
#* For I '''reckon''' that the sufferings of this present time are not worthy to be compared with the glory which shall be revealed in us. --''Romans 8:18, King James Version.''
#* Likewise '''reckon''' ye also yourselves to be dead indeed unto sin... --''Romans 6:11, King James Version''
#* I '''reckon''' he won't try that again.
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To make an enumeration or computation; to engage in numbering or computing.
# To come to an accounting; to make up accounts; to [[settle]]; to examine and strike the balance of debt and credit; to adjust relations of desert or penalty.
#* Parfay," sayst thou, sometime he '''reckon''' shall." [[w:Chaucer|Chaucer]].

